   Can the signals in use today point a generator to these two repairs? The verifier cannot. It accepts
this specification, and it also accepts the empty specification ensures true, which says nothing at
all. FormalBench’s Completeness Rate (CR) goes further. It creates 33 mutants of the method with
Major and counts the mutants for which verification fails. The specification of Fig. 1 gets a CR of
0.36, the empty specification gets 0.09, and an exact postcondition for every branch gets 0.94. CR
can therefore rank these specifications, but it does not say what to fix. Dropping the too-strong
precondition repairs one of the two defects. Yet CR only rises from 0.36 to 0.39. Even this small
gain does not come from the specification. One mutant fails only because OpenJML cannot prove a
range check in the mutated code, and the same failure gives the empty specification its 0.09. CR
also never reaches 1.0 for this method, because two mutants behave exactly like the original. A
generator therefore cannot tell when it is done. Finally, if a revision makes the postcondition wrong,
the specification no longer verifies, and CR is not defined at all. Neither signal tells a generator
which clause is wrong or which way to change it. To be useful as feedback, a signal has to separate
the precondition from the postcondition, and correctness (the clause rejects valid behavior) from
completeness (the clause admits invalid behavior).

2     Modeling Peterson’s Algorithm In TLA+
Peterson’s algorithm is a classic, very simple two-process mutual exclusion al-
gorithm. We specify the algorithm in TLA+ and prove that it satisfies mutual
exclusion, meaning that no two processes are in their critical sections at the
same time.1

2.1     From PlusCal To TLA+
